A||||4||Taijiguy||Support from other countries...||||14||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||09-15-2001||06:11 PM||||I've been completely moved by all the support other countries have shown. Hearing the Prime Minister of Britain talk about standing "...shoulder to shoulder...", playing the National Anthem during the changing of the guards (that's really big), Canadians singing it at various functions, all those things have touched my heart. Surprisingly, the one thing that most quickly brought a tear to my eye was a picture I saw of a memorial service in Germany, they showed a young boy, on his fathers shoulders holding- seemingly quite proudly- an American flag. I thought what a wonderful thing that is, that people in other countries will <I>fly our flag!</I> Usually when you see foreigners holding our flag, it's to set it on fire. That little boy, most certainly without even realizing it, has made a hugely profound statement. <P>I know there are other people in this forum from other countries, and in an effort to make some small gesture of my own, I would like to personally send my thanks to you. Your kind thoughts, words, and gestures make more of a difference than you can possibly know. I can safely say, I'll remember that little boy and his flag for the rest of my life.||||reg||14
Z||000001||Mach10||09-15-2001||06:19 PM||||For the second time this week, even *I* was moved to tears at the footage of memorial services everywhere. The one that got me was the service held way up north in the Inuit reserves. These are people that may NEVER have even SEEN an american, yet they were crying and praying. I'm REALLY proud of our people. <BR>Jean Chretien is right. We will stand right beside our brothers when the time comes to do what is right and just.<P>God bless America.<BR><P>------------------<BR>"Duct Tape is Good to Mach 3"<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.escape.ca/~fogg/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000002||hugh||09-15-2001||07:28 PM||||I've only been to Canada once,and it is a beautiful country.I've never considered Canada another country,just a northern neighborhood of the lower 48,as I'm sure most Canadians consider the lower 48 their southern neighborhood.All Americans who can remember canada getting our hostages out of Iran will always consider them true friends!<BR>One more thing!<BR> There was the CEO of the largest bond company in the world interviewed by Connie Chung on TV the other night.He lost 700 employees in that nightmare.He cried with no shame in front of a nationwide audience and as he talked or tried to talk in response to questions my emotions were almost equal to his.He didn't care about anything except his people.I have never considered anyone better than me as a person,but I will always look up to him as the kind of person all of us should aspire to become.  ||||reg||14
Z||000003||maryjane||09-15-2001||08:43 PM||||I mentioned early in the week "we're fixing to find out who our friends are". Well, we have and looks like, along with our old staunch allies, we have a few new ones.<BR>THANKS TO ALL!||||reg||14
Z||000004||MsBeth||09-17-2001||12:56 AM||||<I>There was the CEO of the largest bond company in the world interviewed by Connie Chung on TV the other night.He lost 700 employees in that nightmare.He cried with no shame in front of a nationwide audience and as he talked or tried to talk in response to questions my emotions were almost equal to his.He didn't care about anything except his people.I have never considered anyone better than me as a person,but I will always look up to him as the kind of person all of us should aspire to become. </I><P>I still have visions of this man in my head ... I totally agree with you ...we should all be more like this man - he is a credit to all mankind. <P><BR>MsBeth||||reg||1
